{"reference":"2 Kings 19","book":"2 Kings","chapter":19,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Assyrian Record","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"An Assyrian royal inscription independently connects Sennacherib, Hezekiah, Judah, tribute, and the crisis described in 2 Kings 18–19. Its account claims victories over fortified Judahite cities, Hezekiah's confinement in Jerusalem, and tribute sent to Nineveh, but it does not claim that Jerusalem was captured. This overlap is historically important, yet the inscription is an official Assyrian victory account rather than a neutral transcript. Its silence about Jerusalem's capture cannot by itself establish how the city survived in Kings; the two accounts share a setting while presenting it from different perspectives.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 19:1-37"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_019_4998b25a714ddc3e4c373265","reader_path_2_kings_019_f59bc8f988659adcb8e3dd35"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}